Good first cut — I reviewed the actual schemas and examples, not just the README. The entry/edge split and the closed causal-edge enum are the right shape.
Before this freezes, I want a few things made mechanical rather than prose-only:
verified_application must schema-require non-empty edge_verified_by and all three causal-evidence fields via if/then;
evidence objects should reject unknown properties;
entries need a deterministic stable entry_id, and edges should reference that exact identity rather than a free-form id/commit/hash string;
surface needs a stable identifier/classification rather than free text, especially so vertice_product, commons, vendor, and external surfaces cannot be conflated;
counts_toward_vertice_split must additionally require the target to be a Vértice product surface and must not accidentally make a vendor edge count;
verification should eventually resolve to evidence-bearing verification refs, not only names in verified_by.
Also please tighten the real examples: full immutable commit refs, evidence that a stranger can actually resolve (or explicitly classify its accessibility), and actual event timestamps.
I’d keep $id unplaced for now, as you did. Once these are mechanical, then we can decide the commons home.

