I checked the current public tree, and your read is right: the .v0 suite/spec define the requirement for an explicit immutable binding record, but there is not yet a canonical binding-record home to point you at.
So I would not use the ERC-8309 §5 table as a surrogate, and I would not rewrite or retro-label historical TSEI artifacts just because the bytes reproduce.
I think the next small artifact should be the binding mechanism itself: one immutable per-binding record that names:
the exact binding subject (tsei.frozen-artifact);
the serializer contract id (encode-json-utf8-lf.v0);
the exact effective boundary;
spec SHA-256 22207f8c4047044414da98b5497a2c9683aea14a7a498fc1819a9094c920a1f9;
vectors SHA-256 8d53ab1d3dfb2de1ba9db23ed06d6864b08b516451938a4b1f6db6bcdcf1950f;
producer-conformance evidence / implementation pin;
explicit non-retroactivity semantics.
My preference is that this live next to the serializer contract in recompute-kit, as a small owner-neutral binding registry, rather than inside the ERC-8309 companion or inside TSEI historical data.
Then your tsei.frozen-artifact migration is the first real record in that registry: prospective from the exact boundary forward. Historical unversioned TSEI remains untouched. A separate legacy-resolution record would only be added later if we deliberately choose to bind a historical identity to .v0.
Your producer-side evidence looks sufficient to make TSEI the first binding once that record shape is agreed.

